Let's start with thinking about what would happen if we kept $A$ constant and changed $B$.

The expression has two factors, and only one of them depends on $B$.

$\begin{align}
\boxed{A+7}&\cdot\boxed{2^B}
\\
\nearrow\quad\quad&\phantom{\cdot}\quad\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{doesn't depend on B}\quad&\quad\text{depends on B}
\end{align}$

What happens to $2^B$ when we increase or decrease $B$'s value?

Only one of the factors depends on $A$:

$\begin{align}
\boxed{A+7}&\cdot\boxed{2^B}
\\
\nearrow\quad\quad&\phantom{\cdot}\quad\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{depends on A}\quad&\quad\text{doesn't depend on A}
\end{align}$

When we increase $A$, the value of $A+7$ increases as well. This, in turn, will increase the value of the entire expression.
